{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792709477,"nonce":"KikrR9/9ahpu0FUccadI5Xk3XK0=","title":"Static layer groups lose isolation when a descendant enters the motion batch","message":"Scoped child of e0a7502e3e9d71bb983572df7db0033a67da1c48262e53368ae2399bc22e583a.\n\nA group opened in the static batch can contain both static siblings and a motion-bound descendant. Entering that descendant routes native shapes into the dynamic batch, outside the ancestor's commands. Flattened alpha cannot preserve overlapping group opacity.\n\nInvestigate retaining native static/motion background content in the ancestor group's batch, suppressing independent promotion across that boundary and preventing foreign cached motion ranges from reaching the dynamic patcher. This deliberately trades cached in-place motion updates for repaint within such groups. Separate foreground, canvas, text/SVG/ImageElement and other cross-stream cases remain on the parent.\n\nAdd scoped producer and offscreen public-entry controls before wiring. Keep checks limited to touched files and directly relevant tests with CARGO_BUILD_JOBS=2. No push authorized.","files":null}]}